    deepLimit: "Placebo effects are not fake to the person feeling them. In health especially, belief can matter. The practical question is whether paying more is the best way to get the benefit.",
    fullScene: "Imogen stands at the pharmacy counter at 1:03 p.m., tapping the glass above two cold medicines with the same active ingredient. The brand box is glossy and twice the price. Her sinuses hurt. The brand feels more serious because suffering wants a serious answer.",
    fullMechanism: "A price tag can do two jobs. It can signal real quality, and it can shape expectation. When the result is subjective, like pain, taste, comfort, or confidence, the expectation may become part of what happens next.",
    fullSecond: "Belinda sees it in the gym. Members praise the new premium recovery drink after a price increase, though the formula did not change. The higher price gives them a story for why their legs feel better.",
    fullObjection: "Cheap is not always equal. Quality, safety, durability, and support can cost money. The error is using price as proof when a simpler test is available.",
    fullMove: "Compare ingredients, blind labels, and track outcomes. If the expensive option keeps winning, the price may be buying real value. If it only wins when you can see the box, decide whether the belief itself is worth the cost.",
